package android.hardware.identity_credential@1.0;

import IWritableIdentityCredential;
import IIdentityCredential;

/**
 * IIdentityCredentialStore provides an interface to a secure store for user identity documents.
 * This HAL is deliberately fairly general and abstract.  To the extent possible, specification of
 * the message formats and semantics of communication with credential verification devices and
 * issuing authorities (IAs) is out of scope for this HAL.  It provides the interface with secure
 * storage but a credential-specific Android application will be required to implement the
 * presentation and verification protocols and processes appropriate for the specific credential
 * type.
 *
 * The design of this HAL makes few assumptions about the underlying secure hardware.  In particular
 * it does not assume that the secure hardware has any storage beyond that needed for a persistent,
 * hardware-bound AES-256 key.  However, its design allows the use of secure hardware that does have
 * storage, specifically to enable "direct access".  Most often, Credentials will be accessed
 * through this HAL and through the Android system layer above it, but that requires that the
 * Android device be powered up and fully functional.  It is desirable to allow identity credential
 * usage in the case where the Android device's battery is too low to boot the Android operating
 * system.  In this case, direct access to the secure hardware via NFC may allow data retrieval, if
 * the secure hardware chooses to implement it.  Definition of how data is retrieved in low power
 * mode is explicitly out of scope for this HAL specification; it's up to the relevant identity
 * credential standards to define.
 *
 * Multiple Credentials can be created.  Each Credential comprises:
 *
 * - A set of name/value pairs, each with an associated set of access control profile IDs and an
 *   issuer signature.  Names are unstructured byte sequences of up to 256 bytes in length (most
 *   should be much shorter).  Values are typed and are either bytestrings of up to 2**20 bytes in
 *   length or signed 16, 32 or 64-bit integers.
 *
 * - A set of access control profiles, each with a profile ID and a specification of the
 *   conditions which satisfy the profile's requirements.
 *
 * - An assymmetric key pair which is used to authenticate the Credential to the IA.
 *
 * - A set of zero or more named reader authentication public keys, which are used to authenticate
 *   an authorized reader to the identity credential.
 *
 * - A set of named signing keys, which are used to sign collections of values and session
 *   transcripts.
 *
 * - An optional credential type string.  This is a hint to secure hardware that may offer access to
 *   the same credential data through an alternative interface.
 *
 * Cryptographic notation:  Throughout this HAL, cryptographic operations are specified in detail.
 * To avoid repeating the definition of the notation, it's specified here.  It is assumed that the
 * reader is familiar with standard cryptographic algorithms and constructs.
 *
 *     AES-GCM-ENC(K, N, D, A) represents AES-GCM encryption with key 'K', nonce 'N', additional
 *         authenticated data 'A' and data 'D'.  The nonce is usually specified as 'R', meaning 12
 *         random bytes.  The nonce is always 12 bytes and is prepended to the ciphertext. The GCM
 *         tag is 16 bytes, appended to the ciphertext.  AES-GCM-DEC with the same argument notation
 *         represents the corresponding decyrption operation.
 *
 *    || represents concatenation
 *
 *    {} represents an empty input; 0 bytes of data.
 *
 *    HBK represents a device-unique, hardware-bound AES-256 key which exists only in secure
 *        hardware, except for "test" credential stores (see createStore, below).  For test stores,
 *        an all-zero value is used in place of the HBK.
 *
 * Data encoding notation: Various fields need to be encoded as precisely-specified byte arrays.
 * Where existing standards define appropriate encodings, those are used.  For example, X.509
 * certificates.  Where new encodings are needed, CBOR is used.  CBOR maps are described in CDDL
 * notation (https://tools.ietf.org/html/draft-ietf-cbor-cddl-05).
 *
 * TODO(swillden): Decide if we need to add cryptographic algorithm agility across the board (seems
 * like a good idea) and whether we need to support multiple algorithms now (prefer to avoid).
 * Right now this HAL uses AES-GCM for all encryption and data authentication, ECDSA for all signing
 * and ECDH for all key agreement.  The curve or curves need to be specified as well.
 */
interface IIdentityCredentialStore {

    /**
     * createCredential creates a new Credential.  When a Credential is created, two cryptographic
     * keys are created: CredentialSecret, an AES key used to secure the externalized Credential
     * contents, and CredentialKeyPair, an EC key pair used to authenticate the store to the IA.  In
     * addition, all of the Credential data content is imported and a certificate for the
     * CredentialKeyPair and a signature produced with the CredentialKeyPair are created.  These
     * latter values may be checked by an issuing authority to verify that the data was imported
     * into secure hardware and that it was imported unmodified.
     *
     * @param credentialType is an optional name (may be an empty string) that identifies the type
     *     of credential being created, e.g. org.iso.18013 (ISO 18013 is the ISO driving license
     *     standard).  It serves as a hint to secure hardware that may offer the Credential data
     *     through another interface.
     *
     * @param testCredential indicates if this is a test store.  Test credentials must use an
     *     all-zeros hardware-bound key (HBK) and must set the test bit in the
     *     credentialCertificate.
     *
     * @return entryBlobs is a vector of byte vectors, one for each entry in the credential.
     */
    createCredential(string credentialType, bool testCredential)
        generates(Error error, IWritableIdentityCredential writableCredential);

    /**
     * getCredential retrieves an IIdentityCredential HIDL interface which allows use of a stored
     * Credential.
     *
     * @param credentialBlob is an encrypted byte array that contains data used to secure the
     *     credential.  See the return argument of the same name in createCredential().
     *
     * @return credential is an IIdentityCredential HIDL interface that provides operations on the
     *     Credential.
     */
    getCredential(vec<uint8_t> credentialBlob)
	generates (Error error, IIdentityCredential credential);
};
